## Resource Naming

By default, this chart uses release-name based resource naming for historical reasons and
backwards compatibility with existing deployments.

> [!WARNING]
> For new deployments, it is recommended to set `useLegacyNaming: false` to use operator-style
> names (e.g. `vtsingle-<release>`). This aligns resource names with the
> [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) convention and
> simplifies future migration to operator-managed resources.

### VictoriaMetrics operator

With `operator.enabled: true` the chart renders a [VTSingle](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/resources/vtsingle/) resource
instead of its own Deployment/StatefulSet, Service and ServiceAccount,
and the [VictoriaMetrics operator](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/operator/) manages the workload.
The operator must already be installed in the cluster. Operator mode always uses operator-style names, regardless of `useLegacyNaming`.

The VTSingle is named after the release (or `fullnameOverride`), so the operator creates `vtsingle-<release>` objects.
Ingress and HTTPRoute are still rendered by the chart and target them. The operator runs the VTSingle as a single pod, so `podDisruptionBudget.enabled` and `server.replicaCount` greater than 1 fail rendering.

The spec is built from the `server` values: image, replicas, resources, scheduling, env, extra args, volumes, containers,
pod metadata, security context, HTTP listen port, retention, Service settings, service account and license. In addition:

- `networkPolicy` ingress and egress rules become the resource `networkPolicy`, and the operator creates the NetworkPolicy. Its labels and annotations can't be set;
- the chart keeps managing the `server.persistentVolume` claim (or uses `existingClaim`) and passes it to the VTSingle as its data volume,
  mounted at `server.persistentVolume.mountPath`. A release that ran with a chart-managed claim keeps its data after switching to the operator;
- `server.vmServiceScrape.enabled` or `server.serviceMonitor.enabled` makes the operator create the VMServiceScrape for VictoriaTraces, instead of the chart rendering its own scrape object.

Fields that have no chart value, or need a different value, go to `operator.spec`, which is deep-merged over the generated spec:

```yaml
operator:
  enabled: true
  spec:
    logLevel: WARN
    useStrictSecurity: true
```

Probes keep the operator defaults. Switching a release to `operator.enabled: true` recreates its pod.

